#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321116 :question/problem "![](images/ab/abab926e465522d30b236577bf8a1654502807b864c9fed8a4cebb84efffd3a2.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321117 :question/problem "![](images/35/35c62ccf48c4d0a49729da04b443cb94d112e673e0c24e7784d22f48eaabf8f7.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /3} & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321141 :question/problem "![](images/16/16b676776dc22aece5a1fa777007a03b9e7903706cbdf5cc4ae14d8508370419.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r={e}^{2\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{2\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2{e}^{2\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}+{(2{e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4\\theta }+4{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{[{e}^{2\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{2(\\pi /2)}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321143 :question/problem "Find the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r={3}^{\\theta /4}$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 4.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}$\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡3+16}$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={3}^{\\theta /4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={3}^{\\theta /4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{{({3}^{\\theta /4})}^{2}+{({3}^{\\theta /4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{{3}^{\\theta /2}+{3}^{\\theta /2}\\cdot \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}){3}^{\\theta /2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{3}^{\\theta /4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {3}^{\\theta /4}\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({3}^{1}-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡3+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323711 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ for $f(x,y)=(x+1)^{{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+1)^{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+1) & ={y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\cdot (1+0) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323712 :question/problem "Find ${g}_{z}(x,y,z)$ for $g(x,y,z)=(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+(z+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+(z+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((z+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z+y) \\\\  & =(x+y)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323713 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323732 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Unfortunately, this limit takes the indeterminate form because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)^{2}}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot (-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy-xy-{y}^{2}}{z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)-y(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)} & =\\frac{2(1)-(-1)}{1(1-(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323738 :question/problem "Evaluate $∇h(x,y)$ at $(1,1)$ if $h(x,y)=4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =⟨{h}_{x}(x,y),\\,{h}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y,\\,\\frac{4x}{{y}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\ ∇h(1,1) & =⟨4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1,\\,\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{4}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi ,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323740 :question/problem "Evaluate the gradient of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ if $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),\\,{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟨x,\\,y⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\,0) & =\\frac{2}{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{0}^{2}}⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\,\\frac{1}{4}}⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323752 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}=$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,1,0)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,1,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(1)(0)+(0)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(1)+(-1)(0)-(1)(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0+0}{1+1+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323753 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first factoring the denominator, and then canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xy-xy-2{y}^{2}}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2x(x+2y)-y(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x} & =\\frac{1+2\\cdot 2}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323770 :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}t,\\operatorname{cos}t⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{x}^{2},2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =⟨3{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t,2\\operatorname{cos}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,-\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t,2\\operatorname{cos}t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,-\\operatorname{sin}t⟩ \\\\  & =3{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\  & =3{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\operatorname{cos}t-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))] & =3{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323790 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y$ at the point $A(1,0)$ in the direction towards the point $B(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)=\\mathrm{∇}f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨0,1⟩-⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,0) & =⟨{e}^{0}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 0,\\,1\\cdot {e}^{0}+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,0) & =\\mathrm{∇}f(1,0)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323791 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2xy+{e}^{3x-y}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-(2y+3{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-(2x-{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\ -\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(1,3) & =-(2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot {e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 1-{e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-9\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction of $-∇f(1,3)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(1,3)‖ & =‖-9\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{82}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323792 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,0,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+4y+x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,0,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)} & =(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+4(0)+(-2) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-8 & =-4(y-0) \\\\ z & =-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,0,8)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=-4y+8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323794 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point  where $(x,y)=(1,1)$, can be found by computing $∇g(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\,i+\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,i+\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇g(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}g(1,1)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}g(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323795 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2xy+6{y}^{2}=x-2.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(8,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy+6{y}^{2} & =x-2 \\\\ 2xy+6{y}^{2}-x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy+6{y}^{2}-x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(8,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(8,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(8,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-1)\\,i+(2x+12y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(8,-3) & =(2(-3)-1)\\,i+(2(8)+12(-3))\\,j \\\\  & =-7\\,i-20\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-7,-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(8,-3)=⟨-7,-20⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(8,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-8}{-7} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-20} \\\\ -20(x-8) & =-7(y+3) \\\\ -20x+7y+160+21 & =0 \\\\ -20x+7y+181 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323798 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{3/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}},\\,0<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\right)}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3/t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3({1}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(5)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15\\sqrt{5}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323802 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Given that the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=y+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(0.5,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡((0.5)(2))+\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =4(y-2) \\\\ z & =4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0.5,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=4y-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323821 :question/problem "![](images/7e/7ead42d83a971733b601f45dcc1ad545a52298fc8a800ba69344651a54f4830d.png)\n\nThe area of the acute triangle $△ABC$ is $20\\sqrt{2}{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Then, the measure of $∠A$ is{{field-1}} ${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=8\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ b & =AC=10\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $m∠A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ 20\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ 20\\sqrt{2} & =40\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ \\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{40} & =\\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}A & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323855 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $Q(1,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $Q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =\\frac{2(1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $Q(1,1)$ is the $1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 2y & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $Q(1,1)$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323860 :question/problem "Evaluate $∇f(x,y)$ at $(2,1)$ if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\sqrt{y+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y+3}) \\\\  & =2x\\sqrt{y+3} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y+3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x\\sqrt{y+3},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y+3}}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =⟨2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{1+3},\\,\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{1+3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323861 :question/problem "Given  $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})$, find $∇g(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(\\frac{1}{y}-2y\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}))\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323868 :question/problem "Evaluate $∇f(x,y,z)$ at the point $(2,3,1)$ if $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{xyz}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{xyz})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot yz \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{xyz})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot xz \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{xyz})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot xy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2z}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323869 :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,i+2yz\\,j+{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{i}}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{j}}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{k}}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (2yz)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323874 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x$, in what direction does the function increase most rapidly at the point $(1,0,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x+y),\\,6xz⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1+0)+3\\cdot {1}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1+0),\\,6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ),\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323875 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y).$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}(x+y))\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}(x+y))\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}(x+y)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{ln}(x+y)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\ -\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}(2-1)+1)\\mathbf{i}-(\\operatorname{ln}(2-1)+1)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323878 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=2x-x{y}^{2}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=3{t}^{2}$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+6t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =((2-9{t}^{4})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+6t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+(-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}t)\\cdot 6t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323880 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=(2{t}^{2}+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{arctan}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2{t}^{2}+2$ and $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}t}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot 4t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t\\operatorname{arctan}t+1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(1))] & =\\frac{2(1)\\operatorname{arctan}1+1}{({1}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323883 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=0$ given that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=y(\\operatorname{ln}x+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{ln}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)={e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}(2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=y(\\operatorname{ln}x+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{ln}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={e}^{2t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}(2t)(\\operatorname{ln}{e}^{2t}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(2t)(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}(2t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{cos}(2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}(2t)(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (2{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{cos}(2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}(2t)+4t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}(2t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}[(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}(2t)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(0))] & =2{e}^{2(0)}((2(0)+1)\\operatorname{sin}(2(0))+2(0)\\operatorname{cos}(2(0)))=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323885 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y-5yz,$ where $x=st,$ $y={s}^{3}$ and $z=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}-5z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(st)({s}^{3})=2{s}^{4}t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(st)^{2}-5(s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =-5{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3{s}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(2{s}^{4}t)\\cdot t+({s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3{s}^{2}+(-5{s}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}+3{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-15{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323886 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-2y,$ where $x=s{t}^{2}$ and $y={s}^{3}-t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =({s}^{3}-t)^{2}={s}^{6}-2{s}^{3}t+{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2(s{t}^{2})({s}^{3}-t)-2=2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-2s{t}^{3}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & ={t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =({s}^{6}-2{s}^{3}t+{t}^{2})({t}^{2})+(2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-2s{t}^{3}-2)(3{s}^{2}) \\\\  & ={s}^{6}{t}^{2}-2{s}^{3}{t}^{3}+{t}^{4}+6{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-6{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2} \\\\  & =7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323918 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=8xy+2-8e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =8xy+2-8e \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy & =2-8e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2-8e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(1,e)={f}_{y}(1,e)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(1,e)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x}{y}-8x)i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8y)\\,j \\\\ t(1,e) & =(\\frac{2(1)}{e}-8(1))i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-8(e))\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $(\\frac{2}{e}-8)\\mathbf{i}+(8e-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323974 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(32-0)+1(0-0)+2(0-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323975 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -4\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(4-0)-0+0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323976 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta ))^{2}}{2}-0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(2\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(2\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}(4\\theta ))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}(4\\theta )]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}(-2\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326669 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & =(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1(-20-0)-0+3(0-5) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-20)+3\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =20-15 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326670 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =6\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+7\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6(-2-0)-0+7(0-2) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-2)+7\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-12-14 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326671 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $E$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 5 & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 5 & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 8\\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(40-42)-3(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{12}{3})+4(\\frac{14}{3}-\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{12}{3}+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(E)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$E$ is a singular matrix” is true."}]}
